@Hammer power-up: EXPERIMENTAL hooks. Not loaded by the default build.
@
@ ===========================================================================
@ WHY THESE ARE SEPARATE
@ ===========================================================================
@ Everything else in sweetdx/hooks/ is derived from the reference build and, where
@ testable, verified byte-exact. These four are the exception: they are plausible and
@ they came from the same diff, but I cannot demonstrate that they are correct, and two
@ of them are known to have side effects on ordinary gameplay.
@
@ To include them, copy this file to sweetdx/hooks/ (SweetDX globs that directory).
@ Expect Fire Mario to misbehave while it is loaded. See the notes per hook.
@
@ ===========================================================================
@ WHAT THE TEST RUN SHOWED
@ ===========================================================================
@ A build containing these hooks was tested in game. Result: significant rendering
@ problems, and a crash on touching the Hammer power-up.
@
@ Since then two concrete faults have been found and fixed, and they were NOT in this
@ file:
@   * pukupuku.cpp was being compiled, and its file-scope Profile objects used RETAIL
@     ids 530/539. Profile's constructor does `Profiles[id] = this` unconditionally, so
@     loading subsdk0 replaced the game's own Pukupuku (Cheep Cheep) profiles. That is a
@     world-wide defect and an excellent candidate for the rendering corruption at level
@     load. The file now lives in sweetdx/findings/ and is not compiled.
@   * the six mode-table reader words in mode_tables.hks are PC-relative offsets, not
@     addresses. They were briefly (wrongly) removed; they are restored and verified.
@
@ So the two fixes above may well account for both symptoms. These hooks are still the
@ least-supported part of the set, which is why they are quarantined rather than shipped.

grant_hammer_mode:
    @ Granting the Hammer gives power-up mode 10.
    @
    @ CONFIRMED CORRECT. Retail has `mov r2,#2` at this address; the reference build has
    @ `mov r2,#0xa`. Together with the 0x1FB->0x3FB mask change in item_grant.hks this is
    @ what makes mode 10 reachable at all. This one is safe and is a candidate to promote
    @ into a shipped file.
    type:    instr
    instruction: mov r2, #0xa
    address: 0x0045DDCC

hammer_actor_info_flags:
    @ Second ActorInfo record: clear the fire-specific flag word.
    @
    @ UNCERTAIN. This sits inside a global ActorInfo record (neighbours are 12, then
    @ floats 1.75/2.75/1.0/-1.0/-3.0), and it has zero direct xrefs, so it is reached only
    @ through a table. Writing 0 here affects whatever else shares that record. Editing a
    @ shared global is exactly how you get corruption far from the feature you meant to
    @ change, so this needs per-field justification before shipping.
    type:    data
    data:    00 00 00 00
    address: 0x00AF6620

hammer_actor_info_subtype:
    @ ActorInfo subtype -> 19.
    @
    @ UNCERTAIN, and note the alignment. Retail has 0 at 0xBD3208 and 0x0000824F at
    @ 0xBD320C; our write is 4 bytes at 0xBD3208, which is a clean word slot, so the write
    @ itself is aligned. But 19 is a magic value I cannot tie to anything: nothing in the
    @ image names subtype 19, and no source confirms "19 means projectile-like". Treat as
    @ a guess until shown otherwise.
    type:    data
    data:    13 00 00 00
    address: 0x00BD3208

hammer_actor_info_dist:
    @ ActorInfo hit distances.
    @
    @ UNCERTAIN. Retail word is 0x00088200, ours is 0x00080001 -- a small change in a
    @ packed field, so the two halves mean different things and I have not decoded them.
    @ Plausible, unproven.
    type:    data
    data:    01 00 08 00
    address: 0x00BD3210

hammer_profile_cell:
    @ Point the power-up slot at profile 759 instead of the Fire Flower's 476.
    @
    @ DO NOT SHIP. This is the hook I understand best and trust least.
    @
    @ What the cell actually is (now established, after two wrong theories):
    @   * [0xC61BE4] holds a pointer. Retail = 0x00AF3498, ours = 0x00AF3904.
    @   * The region 0xAF2D28..0xAF3B60 is ONE run of 911 consecutive integers 0..910, so
    @     element n holds the value n.
    @   * Retail therefore reads 476, ours reads 759. There is no table of tables; 37 of
    @     40 access sites do a plain LDR at offset 0, with no index register.
    @   * Readers compare that value against Actor::getProfileId(actor), i.e. it answers
    @     "is this actor the Fire Flower?" (confirmed in sub_6000A2E8: v8 = *cell, then
    @     `result = Actor::getProfileId(*v4); v10 = result == v8;`).
    @   * 759 is inside 0..910, and there are exactly 911 Profile constructor call sites,
    @     so 759 is a registered profile -- it is not a null slot. An earlier claim of mine
    @     that get(759) returns NULL was wrong.
    @
    @ So this hook does not add a Hammer slot. It REPLACES the Fire Flower identity
    @ everywhere: Fire Mario stops being recognised by the fire/projectile code, and that
    @ code is simultaneously told to treat profile 759 as fire. Mode 10 is already enabled
    @ by item_grant.hks and 0x45DDCC without this, so it buys nothing.
    @
    @ Kept only so the experiment is reproducible -- never in the default build.
    type:    data
    data:    04 39 AF 00
    address: 0x00C61BE4
